<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Masa U'Masan 4</div>
<div class="part-label">Part 1 — Sections 1–5</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Opening Halacha / Subtitle                      -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="halacha-subtitle">
  The matter of business dealings. One should be careful not to enter into a partnership with idolaters, etc., on account of: <span class="verse">"And the name [of other gods] you shall not mention; it shall not be heard,"</span> etc.
  <span class="source-ref">(As explained there in Orach Chaim, Siman 156)</span>
</div>

<div class="lm-subtitle">
  Based on the Torah [teaching]: <span class="verse">"And it was after these things…"</span> etc.
  <span class="source-ref">(<span class="lm-ref">Likutay Moharan I, 76</span>)</span> — see there, the entire Torah [teaching].
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Introduction                                    -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="paragraph">
  It is explained there that <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span> <span class="heb-def">[gazing / contemplative looking]</span> is an aspect of <span class="key-concept"><span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> [direct light] and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> [returning light]</span>, etc. And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">"For Hashem is exalted, yet He sees the lowly, and the haughty He knows from afar"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 138:6)</span>, etc. And through <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span>, a <span class="heb-term">kli</span> <span class="heb-def">[vessel]</span> is formed — that is, <span class="key-concept">boundary and time</span>. For before one sees the thing, it is without boundary. And through the <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span> — by seeing the thing and it becoming engraved in one's mind — through this, a boundary is made for it, etc.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of the virtue of <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> <span class="heb-def">[trust in G-d]</span>. For <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> is an aspect of <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span>, an aspect of: <span class="verse">"The eyes of all look hopefully to You"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 145:15)</span>. And through this, one draws down the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> <span class="heb-def">[influx / sustenance]</span> and livelihood — for the <span class="heb-term">hashpa'ah</span> <span class="heb-def">[Divine flow]</span> descends from Above constantly, except that it is without boundary and time. And it could be that what one needs now might come to him only after many years. But through <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> — which is an aspect of <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span> toward Hashem, blessed be He — through this, one makes a vessel for the <span class="heb-term">hashpa'ah</span>, that is, boundary and time, so that the <span class="heb-term">hashpa'ah</span> should come at the time it is needed.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is [the meaning of]: <span class="verse">"The eyes of all look hopefully to You, and You give them their food <b>in its time</b>"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 145:15–16)</span>. For through the <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span> with <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> toward Hashem, blessed be He — the aspect of "the eyes of all look hopefully to You" — through this: "and You give them their food <b>in its time</b>" — <span class="verse">"in its time"</span> specifically, meaning in the aspect of boundary and time, for the <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span> makes a vessel, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of the virtue of drawing close to <span class="heb-term">tzadikim</span> <span class="heb-def">[righteous individuals]</span>. For they create boundary and time so that the engagement in Torah and <span class="heb-term">avodah</span> <span class="heb-def">[service of G-d]</span> should be done properly, according to one's level, within boundary and within time. This is what King David, peace be upon him, prayed: <span class="verse">"My soul thirsts [for You]… When shall I come and <b>appear</b> before the face of G-d?"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 42:3)</span>. <span class="verse">"And appear"</span> — specifically — that the <span class="heb-term">avodah</span> should be in the aspect of <span class="key-concept">seeing</span>, meaning in the aspect of vessel and boundary and time; see there all of this well.
</div>

<div class="author-note">
  For although this Torah [teaching] is from the language of the <span class="heb-term">chavairim</span> [companions, who transcribed it] and was not written well at all, nevertheless, one who examines and studies it carefully will find there, too, wondrous foundations and novel insights. For every single utterance that came forth from the mouth of <span class="heb-term">Rabbainu</span> [Rebbe Nachman], of blessed memory, is exceedingly deep and awesome.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 1                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 1</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of <span class="maxim">"conducting business dealings with <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Shabbos 31a)</span>. For the primary purpose of business dealings is to draw down the aspect of <span class="key-concept">the power of sight</span>, which is the aspect of His <span class="heb-term">hashgachah</span> <span class="heb-def">[Providence]</span>, blessed be He — that it should be drawn upon us in completeness, in the aspect of <span class="heb-term">re'iyah</span> <span class="heb-def">[seeing]</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>. Through this, a vessel is made for the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span>, so that it should come at the [right] time, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For all business dealings are only completed through two people who conduct business with one another — this one gives that one money or a certain item, and his fellow gives him in exchange a different item. And through this, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> is made: for each one extends his flow to his fellow — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> [direct light] — and afterwards he in turn receives from him — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> [returning light]. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">re'iyah</span>, through which specifically the <span class="heb-term">hashpa'ah</span> is drawn down within boundary and time, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  Therefore, in truth, the primary drawing down of the flow of livelihood is only through <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span>, which is an aspect of <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span> — the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> — through which one makes a vessel, namely, boundary and time, so that the <span class="heb-term">hashpa'ah</span> should be drawn down in boundary and time, and the livelihood should come to him at its proper time and moment. And the fact that one needs to engage in some form of business dealings and commerce is also only for this purpose — to draw down the aspect of the power of sight, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>, through the business dealings, as mentioned above, so that through this the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> should come in boundary and in time, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For certainly Hashem, blessed be He, is able to give livelihood to a person without any cause, without any business dealings whatsoever. As it is written: <span class="verse">"For not from the east or from the west, and not from the wilderness comes greatness; for G-d is the judge"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Psalms 75:7–8)</span>, etc. And as He sustained our forefathers for forty years in the wilderness through the <span class="heb-term">man</span> <span class="heb-def">[manna]</span>, and they lacked nothing at all. Rather, the fact that we now need some form of commerce and business dealings is only because we do not have <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> strong enough to be in the aspect of actual seeing. That is, most [people] do not merit that their eyes should constantly gaze toward the salvation of Hashem alone, with strong <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span>, such that they would draw down through this the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>, through which the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> is drawn down in boundary and time, at its appointed time, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And because not every person merits this now, therefore they need commerce and business dealings. And the primary intention of the business dealings is as mentioned above — to draw down through the business dealings the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>, which is itself the aspect of <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span>. And therefore one must conduct business dealings <span class="key-concept">with <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> specifically</span> — to believe in Hashem that all livelihood is only from Him, blessed be He, and to direct one's eyes to Hashem, blessed be He, alone. For the primary livelihood comes only through this, as mentioned above. And also, the entire purpose of business dealings comes only for this — to make a vessel for the <span class="heb-term">hashpa'ah</span>, namely, boundary and time, so that the <span class="heb-term">hashpa'ah</span> should come in its time, as mentioned above. For the <span class="heb-term">hashpa'ah</span> descends constantly — it is just that it is without boundary and time, etc., as mentioned above. And through the <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span> with <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> — through this, a vessel is made, which is boundary and time, and the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> comes at its appointed time, as mentioned above. And this is itself the aspect of business dealings, which is an aspect of exchange — the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, in truth, whoever has exceedingly strong <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> in Hashem, blessed be He, casts off from himself to a greater degree the yoke of the burden of business dealings — to the point that there are <span class="heb-term">tzadikim</span> who have <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> so strong that they do not need any commerce or business dealings whatsoever, nor any activity or travel at all for the sake of livelihood.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For when one's <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> is exceedingly strong in Hashem, blessed be He, then he makes a vessel for the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> — namely, boundary and time — through the <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> alone, which is the aspect of the power of sight, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>, as mentioned above. And the primary [need for] business dealings is only due to the weakness of <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> in its ultimate completeness. Therefore, one needs business dealings in order to strengthen the vessel to receive the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> — through the business dealings which are an aspect of the power of sight, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>, as mentioned above. But even one who engages in business dealings — nevertheless, the main thing is the <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span>. For without <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> and <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span> toward Hashem, blessed be He, all the business dealings in the world will not help him. For <span class="verse">"the riches and honor are from before Him"</span> <span class="source-ref">(I Chronicles 29:12)</span>, blessed be He. It is only because he lacks the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> <span class="heb-def">[awareness/knowledge]</span> to strengthen himself in <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> alone, without any activity of any commerce or business dealings — and therefore he needs business dealings. But then [during the dealings] his <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> should be even stronger, and then he draws down livelihood by making the vessel of boundary and time, etc., as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 2                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 2</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of what Yirmiyahu [Jeremiah] said to Israel: <span class="verse">"O generation, <b>see</b> the word of Hashem!"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Jeremiah 2:31)</span>. And our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded <span class="source-ref">(Mechilta, Beshalach; Yoma 75a)</span>: "'Hear' was not said, but rather '<b>see</b>' — from here [we learn] that he brought out for them the jar of <span class="heb-term">man</span> [manna]," etc., and he said to them: "See with what your forefathers were sustained," etc.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For Israel in the wilderness were nourished through <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> alone — and this is the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">man</span> that descended for them without any activity or commerce or business dealings whatsoever. And it was all in the merit of Moshe, who is the <span class="heb-term">Da'as</span> [Knowledge/Awareness], and his generation was a <span class="heb-term">dor dai'ah</span> <span class="heb-def">[a generation of knowledge]</span>. For he drew into them such great <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> that they directed the eyes of their awareness to gaze with <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> toward Hashem, blessed be He, alone — until the <span class="heb-term">man</span> descended for them for forty years, day by day.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is the aspect of the trial spoken of in connection with the <span class="heb-term">man</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">"Behold, I will rain down bread for you from Heaven, in order that I may <b>test</b> them"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Exodus 16:4)</span>, etc. For the primary trial was whether they would strengthen themselves in <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> every single day, and not think or entertain any other thought of worry regarding livelihood, saying: "What will we do if the <span class="heb-term">man</span> does not descend tomorrow?" Rather, [the test was] that they should strengthen themselves in <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> every single day — that Hashem, blessed be He, will certainly give them their food in its time.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And as is understood there in the <span class="heb-term">parasha</span> [Torah portion], this was the primary trial of the <span class="heb-term">man</span>; and therefore they were commanded: <span class="verse">"No man shall leave over from it until morning"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Exodus 16:19)</span>. And therefore, indeed there were found among them people who left over from it, and Moshe became angry with them. For the primary descent of the <span class="heb-term">man</span> was through <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> — to trust in Hashem, blessed be He, alone — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span>, through which alone one creates boundary and time for the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span>, through which the livelihood descended for them from Heaven alone.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And similarly, our Sages, of blessed memory, said that the reason the <span class="heb-term">man</span> descended for them every day and not all at once for many days was <span class="maxim">"so that they would direct their eyes heavenward"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Yoma 76a)</span> — meaning as mentioned above: so that they would have strong <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And this is what Yirmiyahu said to them — when he wanted them to engage in Torah and strengthen themselves in <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> alone, until the livelihood should come to them from Heaven alone, without any commerce, just as the <span class="heb-term">man</span> descended for our forefathers — therefore he said to them: <span class="verse">"<b>See</b> the word of Hashem."</span> <span class="verse">"See"</span> — specifically. Meaning, he informed them that the primary drawing down of livelihood is through <span class="key-concept">the power of sight alone</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span>. For through a person gazing with <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> only toward Hashem, blessed be He, alone — he is able to draw down livelihood at its appointed time and moment, without any cause or commerce at all. Rather, the livelihood comes to him from Heaven, just like the actual descent of the <span class="heb-term">man</span>, which was only through <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 3                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 3</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  It emerges that the business dealings with <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of Israel serve only to draw down <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> — through which a vessel is made. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span>, for the primary purpose of business dealings is only to strengthen the vessel that is made through <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span>, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore it is forbidden to enter into a partnership with idolaters, on account of: <span class="verse">"And the name of other gods you shall not mention"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Exodus 23:13)</span>. For the primary vessel — namely, that the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> should descend in boundary and time — this is made through the power of sight, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>. And the primary aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> that is made through the power of sight is only when one directs and contracts the sight toward a specific thing upon which one gazes. (And as is explained in the Torah [teaching] mentioned above, and in the Torah [teaching] <span class="verse">"And Boaz said to Ruth"</span> [<span class="lm-ref">Likutay Moharan I, 65</span>, see there], and in the Torah [teaching] <span class="verse">"Happy is the people"</span> — which discusses <span class="heb-term">hashgachah</span> [Providence] — [<span class="lm-ref">Likutay Moharan I, 13</span>, see there].)
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And the principle is: one cannot truly see a thing — [meaning] to take the thing into one's awareness, that is, to form it in one's mind — unless the power of sight travels in a directed manner toward that thing and strikes it [<span class="heb-term">haka'ah</span>, impact]. Then the sight returns to his eyes through that impact, and the thing becomes depicted in his eyes. And then he sees the thing. And this is itself the aspect of the power of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> that is drawn through the power of sight, as explained in the Torah [teaching] mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore it is forbidden to enter into a partnership with idolaters. For the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> of Israel descends through the path of holiness — because they believe in Hashem, blessed be He, and trust in Him alone, and they know that all <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> and livelihood is from Him, blessed be He. For He, blessed be He, watches with <span class="heb-term">hashgachah pratis</span> <span class="heb-def">[individual Providence]</span> over every single thing in the world. And He sits and sustains and provides, <span class="maxim">from the horns of the <span class="heb-term">re'aim</span> [wild oxen] down to the eggs of lice</span> <span class="source-ref">(Avodah Zarah 3b)</span>. And the more a person cleaves below, so is he treated from Above — meaning, just as we believe that livelihood comes through the Providence of Hashem, blessed be He, alone, so too He, blessed be He, watches over us with complete Providence, in the aspect of the power of sight mentioned above — which comes upon us with impact [<span class="heb-term">haka'ah</span>], and we become depicted in His eyes. And as is explained in the Torah [teaching] "Happy is the People" — regarding Providence, etc., see there. And therefore, the livelihood and <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> descend upon us from Him alone, and the <span class="heb-term">klipos</span> [husks/forces of impurity] have no ability to draw sustenance from there.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  But the idolaters and the wicked heretics do not believe in the Providence of Hashem, blessed be He, in its completeness. And the primary heresy of theirs is regarding the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> [returning light], which is made through the contraction — when one directs the awareness and sight in a focused and contracted manner to the place needed, until it returns with impact to his eyes, as mentioned above. But when the sight becomes scattered from the side and does not come in impact to the thing one wants to see, then one does not see the thing, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And so too with the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> of <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> in Hashem, blessed be He — the primary blemish of the idolaters and the heretics is that they do not direct and contract the eyes of their awareness toward Hashem, blessed be He. For in truth, the great majority of them believe in the existence of Hashem, blessed be He. But the primary heresy of theirs is regarding the <span class="key-concept">individual Providence</span> [<span class="heb-term">hashgachah pratis</span>]. For they say that Hashem, blessed be He, is so great, exalted, lofty, and beyond reach that it is impossible to conceive that Hashem, blessed be He, should watch over this world with individual Providence. And through this, they deny the entire Torah, which depends on this.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  But in truth — <span class="heb-term">afra l'fumaiyhu</span> [may their mouths be filled with dirt]! And may their mouths be filled with gravel! For their hearts are devoid of true <span class="heb-term">da'as</span>. For they deceive themselves as if they are magnifying and exalting His honor, blessed be He — saying that because of the greatness of His exaltedness, blessed be He, it is not fitting, G-d forbid, that He should watch over this lowly world. But may their spirit and soul expire! For they wish to grasp His greatness, blessed be He, and to understand the ways of His conduct, blessed be He? On the contrary — this is the very essence of His greatness and exaltedness! For <span class="maxim">in the place of His greatness, there you find His humility</span> <span class="source-ref">(Megillah 31a)</span>. As it is written: <span class="verse">"For thus says the High and Lofty One, Who dwells forever and Whose Name is Holy: 'I dwell on high and in holiness, <b>and with the crushed and lowly of spirit</b>'"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 57:15)</span>, etc. As our Sages, of blessed memory, said, etc.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For if the greatness of Hashem, blessed be He, were only as much as the comprehension of the human mind — then, G-d forbid, His greatness would have a limit, for the mind of a human being — [mere] flesh and blood — has a limit and a boundary. But in truth, His knowledge is exalted beyond our knowledge. And as it is written: <span class="verse">"For as the heavens are higher than the earth, so are My ways higher than your ways, and My thoughts [higher] than your thoughts"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Isaiah 55:9)</span>. And therefore, in truth — on the contrary! — this is His greatness and exaltedness, blessed be He: that He is exalted and beyond reach, far above the grasp of the human mind, to the point that due to the magnitude of His greatness and exaltedness, He watches with <span class="heb-term">hashgachah pratis</span> over every single thing in the world. As it is written: <span class="verse">"In every place the eyes of Hashem are watching and seeing"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Proverbs 15:3)</span>. And even though we cannot comprehend this with our understanding, we set aside our understanding and believe in this with <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> alone. For this is the true completeness of <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> — to contract the awareness and to understand and comprehend that it is impossible to understand His ways and conduct, blessed be He, and to rely on <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> alone. As our holy forefathers transmitted to us — they who broke their bodies completely and merited to attain perfect <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>. And upon them alone we need to rely, and to walk in their ways.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 3 continued                              -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="paragraph">
  It emerges that Israel, who have perfect <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> and <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> in Hashem, blessed be He — this is the aspect of the power of sight, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>. That is, the light of the eyes of their awareness travels in a straight path [<span class="heb-term">yosher</span>] toward Hashem, blessed be He — for they believe in His existence, blessed be He, in His greatness and in His exaltedness, blessed be He, without end and without limit. But through this very magnification of <span class="heb-term">da'as</span>, the awareness could become scattered, G-d forbid, until it departs from its boundary. And through this, the <span class="heb-term">da'as</span> would become completely nullified — meaning, if they wished, G-d forbid, to understand and grasp the ways of His greatness according to their own understanding, they could come to false opinions and to great heresies, G-d forbid, like the idolaters.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  But Israel — the holy nation — they <span class="key-concept">contract their awareness and direct their minds with great focus toward Hashem, blessed be He, alone</span>. For they know, with perfect <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>, of the greatness of Hashem, blessed be He, and His awesome exaltedness — that He is exalted and beyond reach, exceedingly, exceedingly so, beyond every kind of spirituality in all the worlds, and certainly beyond the physicality of this lowly world. And nevertheless, He, blessed be He, Himself watches with <span class="heb-term">hashgachah pratis</span> over the entire lowly physical world. And He sustains and provides, from the horns of the wild oxen down to the eggs of lice.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And because we direct our awareness toward this holy <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>, in the path that our righteous forefathers transmitted to us — through this, the eyes of our awareness reach Him, blessed be He, Himself, in the aspect of <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span> that is made by reaching that thing in a focused, contracted manner. For we too direct our awareness with great contraction, in the way of <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>, until we reach Him, blessed be He, and draw down His Providence upon us in completeness. For just as we believe in His Providence in specificity, in completeness — so too His Providence is truly drawn upon us in completeness. That is, in the aspect of sight that comes in a focused, straight manner, in the aspect of impact [<span class="heb-term">haka'ah</span>] — until we become depicted in His eyes, in the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ratzo vashuv</span> <span class="heb-def">[running and returning]</span>. And so it goes, revolving each time. And through this, the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> is renewed each time with a great and mighty light, and an abundance of good is drawn to the House of Israel.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 4                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 4</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  But the idolatrous heretics who deny His Providence — because of the foolishness of their mind, saying that He, blessed be He, is too exalted to watch over the world Himself — and from this all their heresies are drawn, and all the forms of idolatry of the earlier generations. For in their folly they say that G-d forbid, Hashem, blessed be He, handed over the governance to the celestial constellation, until they came through this to worship wood and stone, the work of human hands. For they said that it is impossible to draw down the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> except through the worship of a physical image — which is the essence of all their idolatries, as is known from the holy books. For this was the cause of the error of the earlier generations who worshipped idolatry.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And similarly, all the error of the heretics of these generations — it is all through errors like these, because they do not direct and contract their awareness to understand and comprehend that it is impossible to understand the ways of His greatness, blessed be He, and His conduct, blessed be He. And through this, they cannot reach Him, blessed be He, with the eyes of their awareness — like a person who wants to see something from a distance but does not direct and contract his eyes toward that thing, but rather looks from the side; through this, the sight becomes scattered and he cannot see or bring that thing to his eyes. Exactly so is it with the errors of their evil thinking — that they want to gaze at the comprehension of His greatness, blessed be He, but do not direct their awareness to look toward Him, blessed be He, alone; rather, they look from the side, through the paths of their evil philosophical investigations, until they depart from the boundary. And they do not return to draw down His Providence, blessed be He, upon them — because they cannot reach Him, blessed be He, because they have no <span class="heb-term">emunah</span>. For through the intellect alone, it is impossible to attain Him, blessed be He — only through <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> alone. And as is explained in many places, and as mentioned above.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- Section 5                                       -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="section-number">§ 5</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, also Hashem, blessed be He, does not draw upon them complete Providence. And He does not watch over them except in the aspect of <span class="key-concept">"half-sight"</span> [<span class="heb-term">chatzi hare'us</span>], as explained in the Torah [teaching] "Happy is the People" mentioned above. That is, so to speak, the flow of Providence becomes scattered before it reaches them. For the Providence and <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> do not reach them in the aspect of impact [<span class="heb-term">haka'ah</span>]. For <span class="verse">"to look upon iniquity, You cannot"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Habakkuk 1:13)</span>. For just as they do not direct their awareness toward Him to believe in His Providence, blessed be He, so too He, blessed be He, measures for them measure for measure.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And even though in truth, even all the livelihood of the idolaters — everything is from Him, blessed be He, through His Providence alone — nevertheless, He, blessed be He, hands over their <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> to the <span class="heb-term">chitzonim</span> <span class="heb-def">[external forces]</span>. And they are nourished through the path of the <span class="heb-term">shaidim</span> <span class="heb-def">[demons]</span> and the <span class="heb-term">klipos</span> [husks of impurity] that came into being from the shattering of the vessels — which were caused by excess of light. And all of this is because they do not contract their awareness. And their awareness is in the aspect of excess of light that causes shattering of the vessels — for they say that because of His exaltedness and greatness, He, blessed be He, is G-d forbid far from this world. And this is itself the aspect of excess of light that causes shattering of the vessels — for due to the excess <span class="heb-term">da'as</span>, in wanting to grasp the loftiness of His greatness, blessed be He, they split apart and fell completely, until they deny His Providence, blessed be He.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And because of this, Hashem, blessed be He, too watches over them in the aspect of excess light without boundary — meaning, in the aspect of shattering of the vessels. And He does not direct and contract the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span> upon them in impact, but rather <span class="heb-term">basar kasfaih</span> <span class="heb-def">[behind His shoulder, i.e. indirectly]</span>, as found in the Holy Zohar. Through this, all their sustenance descends to the <span class="heb-term">chitzonim</span> and <span class="heb-term">klipos</span> that came into being from excess of light, from shattering of the vessels. And from there they receive their livelihood.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  And therefore, it is forbidden for a Jew to enter into a partnership with idolaters, on account of: <span class="verse">"And the name of other gods you shall not mention"</span>. For the sustenance of the idolaters is impure, because it descends through the path of impurity and the <span class="heb-term">sitra achra</span>, because they do not believe in His Providence in completeness — and this is the aspect of "the name of other gods." For all the heresies in the world are an aspect of idolatry, an aspect of "other gods." And therefore, one who enters into a partnership with them transgresses on account of: "And the name of other gods you shall not mention." For they deny His complete Providence and say: <span class="verse">"My power and the might of my hand have made me this wealth"</span> <span class="source-ref">(Deuteronomy 8:17)</span> — and this is the aspect of idolatry, the aspect of "the name of other gods."
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For we must distance ourselves from them and from their ways to the utmost extreme. For we must believe that all of our livelihood is through Providence alone. And all the business dealings that we do are only in order to draw down the aspect of <span class="heb-term">ohr hayashar</span> and <span class="heb-term">ohr hachozer</span>, in order to make the vessel to receive the <span class="heb-term">shefa</span>. And therefore, for us, the business dealings are not the main thing; rather, the main thing is the <span class="heb-term">emunah</span> and the <span class="heb-term">bitachon</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">histakulus</span> toward Hashem, to believe in His Providence, blessed be He. For all of this is an aspect of the Name of Hashem, as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  For the fact that we contract our awareness to believe in His individual Providence — even though He is exalted and elevated exceedingly, exceedingly — this contraction is the aspect of the <span class="key-concept">Name of Hashem</span>. For <span class="heb-term">shaim</span> [Name] is an aspect of <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span> <span class="heb-def">[Kingship]</span>, an aspect of <span class="heb-term">tzimtzum</span> [contraction], as is known. But the idolaters who deny His individual Providence, and through this come to idolatry and heresies — this is the aspect of <span class="key-concept">"the name of other gods."</span> Therefore, one who enters into a partnership with them transgresses on account of: "And the name of other gods you shall not mention." And as mentioned above.
</div>

<div class="paragraph">
  <em>All these matters, from Section 6 [onward], were only written as chapter-headings. And if Hashem will be with me, they will be explained somewhat.</em>
</div>

