<!-- Translator's Summary — Part 1                   -->
<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<div class="translator-summary">
  <div class="summary-heading">✦ Translator's Summary — Part 1 ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    This lengthy and profound teaching, based on <strong>Likutay Moharan I, 76</strong>, develops a remarkable spiritual theory of economics rooted in the Kabbalistic concepts of <em>ohr hayashar</em> (direct light) and <em>ohr hachozer</em> (returning light).
  </p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    <strong>The core idea:</strong> G-d's sustenance (<em>shefa</em>) flows constantly from Above, but it exists beyond boundary and time. For it to reach us when we need it, a <em>kli</em> (vessel) must be formed — and that vessel is created through <em>histakulus</em> (focused gazing/contemplation), i.e. <em>bitachon</em> (trust in G-d). The mechanism of sight — light traveling outward and returning upon impact — is the paradigm for how Divine Providence operates. Business dealings (<em>masa u'masan</em>) — the give-and-take exchange between two parties — physically mirrors this direct-and-returning light. It is a concession to our weakened <em>bitachon</em>; the manna in the desert, which required no work, represents the ideal.
  </p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.8rem;">
    <strong>Why partnering with idolaters is forbidden:</strong> Israel's <em>emunah</em> in <em>hashgachah pratis</em> (individual Providence) — believing that despite G-d's infinite transcendence, He personally watches over every detail — creates the <em>ohr hachozer</em> that forms a vessel for <em>shefa</em> through holiness. The idolaters' heresy lies in denying Providence: they "look from the side," their awareness scatters, and the returning light never forms. Their sustenance therefore descends through the <em>klipos</em> (impure husks). Partnering with them means connecting one's <em>shefa</em> channel to "other gods."
  </p>

